===== video =====
{
 "file": "tac-7-zte-secret-of-agentic-engineering/video/tac-7-zte-secret-of-agentic-engineering.mp4",
 "duration_seconds": 3200,
 "duration_display": "53:20"
}

===== tactic =====
{
 "name": "Target Zero-Touch Engineering",
 "one_liner": "Build your out-loop system so reliable that human review becomes a bottleneck, not a safety net \u2014 then drop the review: presence goes from two touchpoints (prompt + review) to one (prompt only), and work flows directly from prompt to production.",
 "why_uncomfortable": "TAC-1 asked you to stop coding; TAC-7 asks you to stop reviewing \u2014 the last place engineers feel indispensable. Dan names the reaction in-lesson: 'I know you're probably O-faced right now... Dan, absolutely no way, that's crazy. This isn't a real production application.' He agrees the demo app is a toy, then argues that objection misses the point: ZTE is a North Star you earn one problem class at a time (chores \u2192 bugs \u2192 features), and it only becomes available to engineers who invested in the agentic layer deeply enough to discover their review adds no value. It is 'YOLO mode for your AI developer workflows... maximum confidence mode' \u2014 deliberately provocative, deliberately gated (in the repo, the ZTE workflow literally refuses to run unless 'ZTE' is typed in uppercase).",
 "course_page_definition": "Zero-Touch Engineering is the third level of agentic coding beyond in-loop and out-loop, where your presence KPI drops from two touchpoints (prompt and review) to just one (prompt only). At this level, you witness something most engineers dismiss as impossible - a codebase that nearly runs itself. Your agents ship end-to-end with such reliability that human review becomes a bottleneck, not a safety net, allowing work to flow directly from prompt to production.",
 "daily_actions_from_course_card": [
  "Build parallel agent pipelines using isolated environments",
  "Scale from single agent to multi-agent workflows",
  "Create isolated environments for simultaneous execution",
  "Go from PITER to PITE with one prompt agentic workflows",
  "Build ADWs so great your review step becomes irrelevant"
 ]
}

===== current_practice_gaps =====
[
 {
  "gap": "Hand-rolled worktree orchestration is now a first-class platform feature",
  "why": "tac-7 builds worktree isolation from raw git + port arithmetic + an install prompt. Current Claude Code (mid-2026) exposes isolation natively: worktree-isolated subagents/tasks, sandboxed bash, and cloud/remote agent execution; multi-agent fan-out over worktrees is a standard orchestration pattern rather than custom scripting. The lesson's CONCEPT (isolated, parallel, disposable agent environments with per-run state) transfers unchanged \u2014 the plumbing you'd write today is smaller.",
  "yt_candidates": [
   "fop_yxV-mPo"
  ]
 },
 {
  "gap": "subprocess claude -p pipelines \u2192 Claude Agent SDK",
  "why": "adws/adw_modules/agent.py (subprocess, stream-json parsing, retry ladders, session capture) is exactly the layer the Claude Agent SDK (Python/TS) now provides with structured outputs, hooks, session management, and error semantics. The ADW architecture maps 1:1; a 2026 rebuild would swap agent.py for the SDK and keep every workflow script's shape.",
  "yt_candidates": [
   "6fCqj4xFCZI"
  ]
 },
 {
  "gap": "Reusable engineering as .claude/commands prompt files \u2192 Agent Skills",
  "why": "tac-7 encodes 33 command prompts discovered by convention. Agent Skills (SKILL.md with frontmatter, on-demand loading, composability) are the current first-class packaging for the same idea \u2014 including the conditional_docs.md pattern, which is essentially hand-rolled skill discovery/progressive disclosure.",
  "yt_candidates": [
   "X2ciJedw2vU"
  ]
 },
 {
  "gap": "Model names and model-set economics are period detail",
  "why": "The course-era map is sonnet/opus circa mid-2025 (and the demo app bumps gpt-4.1 \u2192 o4-mini). Model families have turned over since; the durable idea is the base/heavy KNOB \u2014 per-command compute allocation via an IDK stored in state \u2014 not any specific model id. Update the map's values, keep its shape.",
  "yt_candidates": []
 },
 {
  "gap": "ZTE guardrails have matured beyond an uppercase check",
  "why": "The uppercase-ZTE interlock is a charming era artifact of prompt-level safety. Current practice layers deterministic controls around auto-merge: branch protection + required CI checks, permission modes and hooks gating tool use, environment-scoped credentials, staged deploys/canaries, and merge queues. Dan hints at exactly this evolution in-lesson ('add that key deploy check step, a bunch of deterministic deploy checks... parallelize the review step so three agents have to agree \u2014 run the fusion chain on agents').",
  "yt_candidates": []
 },
 {
  "gap": "Review evidence: screenshots \u2192 video + richer review products",
  "why": "The lesson names the gap itself: 'the next most obvious one to build out is video. Have your agents take video, upload it to a cloud bucket, and post the URLs.' The repo's playwright-mcp-config.json already records 1920\u00d71080 video per run, and Dan calls comprehensive review systems 'an entire product category that is important to build out' \u2014 which is precisely what agent-review tooling became.",
  "yt_candidates": []
 }
]

